BIAFYT Context
Purpose

This skill defines the mandatory context-recovery procedure for non-trivial
BIAFYT work.

Its purpose is to prevent implementation based on stale documentation,
assumptions, incomplete context, frontend mocks, planned features, or invented
business rules.

This skill does not define business rules itself. Business/domain rules
belong in BIAFYT_BUSINESS_CORE.md.

This skill does not replace PROJECT_STATE.md, application-specific
CLAUDE.md files, or the actual code and tests.

When to use this skill

Use this skill before:

starting a new product phase;
implementing a feature that crosses domains or applications;
changing business/domain behavior;
changing backend/frontend contracts;
changing ownership, permissions, roles, consent, or authorization;
changing points, streaks, duels, ranking, rewards, programs, workouts,
Coach, Gym, or other core domain behavior;
making architectural decisions;
resolving an apparent contradiction between documentation and code;
continuing work from a previous phase when the current state is not already
established in the conversation.

For small, isolated changes with obvious scope, do not perform unnecessary
context work. Follow the root CLAUDE.md and the applicable local
CLAUDE.md.

Mandatory context order

For non-trivial work, recover context in this order:

1. Business truth

Read:

BIAFYT_BUSINESS_CORE.md

Determine:

what BIAFYT means as a product;
the business/domain concepts involved;
the responsibilities of Usuario/Cliente, Coach, and Gym;
cross-application relationships;
approved business rules;
decisions already made;
decisions explicitly deferred;
constraints and boundaries that must not be violated.

Do not reinterpret an existing business decision without explicit approval.

2. Current project state

Read:

PROJECT_STATE.md

Determine:

current phase;
completed phases;
current implementation status;
roadmap;
known technical debt;
protected files;
known repository anomalies;
backend/frontend integration status;
what is implemented, mocked, shadowed, deferred, or absent.

Do not treat the roadmap as implementation.

Do not treat documentation saying that something exists as proof that it
exists in code.

3. Repository rules

Read:

CLAUDE.md

Then read the applicable application-specific guidance:

backend/CLAUDE.md
frontend-usuario/CLAUDE.md
frontend-couch/CLAUDE.md
frontend-gym/CLAUDE.md

Only read the files relevant to the requested scope when the task is clearly
isolated.

4. Actual implementation

Inspect the relevant:

source code;
routes;
controllers;
models;
migrations;
requests/validation;
API clients;
state management;
components/screens;
tests;
fixtures or mock data;
configuration where relevant.

The actual code and tests determine what the system does today.

Source-of-truth hierarchy

Use the following hierarchy according to the question being answered.

What should the product mean?

Primary authority:

BIAFYT_BUSINESS_CORE.md

What has been decided but not necessarily implemented?

Primary authority:

BIAFYT_BUSINESS_CORE.md + PROJECT_STATE.md

What is supposed to be worked on next?

Primary authority:

PROJECT_STATE.md

What does the system actually do today?

Primary authority:

actual code + tests.

Documentation is supporting evidence only.

How should work be performed?

Primary authority:

CLAUDE.md and the applicable application-specific CLAUDE.md.

Business decision vs implementation status

Always distinguish these states.

A concept may be:

decided but not implemented;
documented but not implemented;
partially implemented;
implemented only in frontend;
implemented only in backend;
implemented as a mock;
implemented as a local-only behavior;
implemented as a best-effort shadow write;
implemented but not authoritative;
explicitly deferred;
absent.

Never collapse these states into "implemented".

For example, a backend model, endpoint, or database table does not prove that
the frontend consumes it.

Likewise, a frontend mock, LocalStorage value, or UI screen does not prove
that the backend domain supports it.

Cross-application model

BIAFYT is one product with three distinct application surfaces:

Usuario/Cliente
Coach
Gym

They share a business domain but have different responsibilities,
permissions, and workflows.

Do not assume that behavior implemented in one application is authoritative
for the entire product.

Business rules that cross application boundaries belong to:

BIAFYT_BUSINESS_CORE.md

Application-specific implementation rules belong in the corresponding
CLAUDE.md.

Anti-hallucination rules

Never:

invent a missing business rule;
invent an endpoint;
invent a model or database field;
invent a role or permission;
invent an authorization relationship;
invent a frontend/backend integration;
assume a mock represents real backend data;
assume a backend model has a frontend consumer;
assume a frontend feature has backend persistence;
assume LocalStorage state is backend-authoritative;
assume a roadmap item is implemented;
assume a planned phase has started;
migrate behavior simply because documentation says it should eventually
migrate;
silently change domain terminology;
silently resolve contradictory business decisions;
silently promote a local or shadow implementation to an authoritative one.

If information is missing, say that it is missing.

If a business rule is ambiguous, stop and request a decision.

If implementation status is unclear, inspect the code and tests before
concluding.

Contradiction protocol

When sources disagree, classify the contradiction before acting.

Case A — Business Core vs implementation

Example:

Business Core says a rule should exist, but code does not implement it.

Conclusion:

the business decision exists;
the implementation is missing.

Do not implement it unless the current task explicitly authorizes the
implementation.

Case B — Business Core vs PROJECT_STATE

Example:

Business Core says a feature is decided, while PROJECT_STATE says it is
deferred.

Conclusion:

the business decision remains valid;
the implementation is deferred.

Do not implement it early.

Case C — PROJECT_STATE vs code

Example:

PROJECT_STATE says a feature exists, but the code does not contain it.

Conclusion:

treat the feature as not currently implemented;
report the documentation drift;
do not assume the missing implementation.
Case D — CLAUDE.md vs code

When determining actual behavior:

code and tests win.

When determining how work should be performed:

applicable CLAUDE.md rules apply unless they contradict a higher-level
business decision or explicit task instruction.
Case E — Business rule is genuinely ambiguous

Stop.

Do not choose the "most reasonable" interpretation silently.

Report:

the ambiguity;
the relevant Business Core section;
the competing interpretations;
what implementation would differ depending on the decision.

Wait for an explicit decision.

Phase boundary rule

A decision being recorded for a future phase does not authorize implementing
it now.

Before changing code, identify:

the current phase;
the requested scope;
the business decision supporting the change;
whether that decision is approved for the current phase;
affected applications;
affected backend domains;
affected contracts;
protected files;
required QA.

If the requested implementation belongs to a later phase, stop and report the
phase-boundary conflict.

Discovery output

For a non-trivial phase or feature, the context-recovery result should be
summarized before implementation.

Use this compact structure:

Business intent

What the Business Core says the product should do.

Current reality

What the code and tests actually do.

Gap

What is missing or inconsistent.

Scope

What the current task explicitly allows changing.

Dependencies

What must exist or be decided first.

Risks / contradictions

Any unresolved issue that could change the implementation.

Decision gate

State one of:

READY TO IMPLEMENT
READY WITH EXPLICIT CONSTRAINTS
BLOCKED — BUSINESS DECISION REQUIRED
BLOCKED — TECHNICAL DEPENDENCY
BLOCKED — SCOPE/PHASE CONFLICT

Do not begin implementation while the decision gate is blocked.

Phase integrity

During a phase:

do not opportunistically implement future roadmap items;
do not "clean up" unrelated architecture;
do not convert mocks into real integrations unless explicitly requested;
do not migrate LocalStorage state to backend authority unless the phase
explicitly authorizes the authority change;
do not add endpoints, migrations, models, or schema changes outside the
approved scope;
do not modify protected files without explicit authorization;
do not touch known legacy repository artifacts when the phase excludes them.

If a useful improvement is discovered but is outside the current phase,
document it and leave it untouched.

Completion check

Before declaring a non-trivial task complete, verify:

The implementation matches the approved business decision.
No deferred decision was implemented early.
No business rule was invented.
No mock was accidentally treated as authoritative.
No frontend/backend authority boundary was changed unintentionally.
Relevant tests were executed.
Documentation was updated only when within scope.
The final state is consistent with PROJECT_STATE.md.
Any remaining contradiction or uncertainty is explicitly reported.

The objective is not merely to make the code pass tests.

The objective is to keep BIAFYT's business model, implementation, and
project state synchronized without silently changing product decisions.

## Phase boundary: technical foundations

A future-phase product may have a technical or visual foundation created
before its product phase, but only when explicitly authorized.

Do not confuse:
- technical/UI scaffolding, and
- business/product implementation.

A UI foundation may include application structure, visual system adaptation,
layout primitives, routing scaffolding and non-domain states.

It must not introduce future-phase business semantics, permissions, domain
models, persistence, endpoints, integrations or workflows.

When a task explicitly says it is a technical/UI foundation and the requested
work does not implement deferred business behavior, evaluate the task on its
actual scope rather than automatically treating it as implementation of the
future product phase.
